<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elberfeld</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elberfeld is a former town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wupper">Wupper</a> valley of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, joined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmen">Barmen</a> and neighbouring towns in 1929 to form the city later named Wuppertal. A centre of linen bleaching and then of textiles and dyeing from the sixteenth century, it was one of the first industrial towns of Germany and in the 1850s gave its name to the Elberfeld system of poor relief.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elberfeld.jpg" alt="Elberfeld" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raimond Spekking · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichsbahndirektion_Elberfeld,_Wuppertal-0113.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Elberfeld lies on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wupper">Wupper</a>. The area is first mentioned, as elverfelde, in a document of 1161. The first part of the name comes from an old Low German word for river, related to the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, so that elverfelde can be read as field on the river. The settlement received its town charter in 1610. Among those born there are the poet Else Lasker-Schüler, the mathematician and physicist Julius Plücker and the conductor Hans Knappertsbusch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1726 Elias Eller and the pastor Daniel Schleyermacher founded a Philadelphian Society in the town. Its members later moved to Ronsdorf in the Duchy of Berg and became the Zionites, a fringe sect. In 1826 the industrialist and politician Friedrich Harkort had a trial suspension railway built and run in Elberfeld, on ground now occupied by the tax office. The Schwebebahn Wuppertal that was eventually built between Oberbarmen and Vohwinkel runs through the town.</p><p>In 1943 the Nazi regime sent forced labourers of the 3rd SS construction brigade to Barmen-Elberfeld. Wuppertal-Elberfeld is regarded as the city most devastated by the Allied strategic bombing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. In a single primary raid 1,795 tons of explosives were dropped, destroying 352 hectares of built-up area, a level of devastation of 94 percent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The district of Sonnborn was incorporated into Elberfeld in 1888. In 1929 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmen">Barmen</a>, Elberfeld, Vohwinkel, Cronenberg and Ronsdorf were united as a single municipality officially called Barmen-Elberfeld, and the council of the new city voted to rename it Wuppertal, a change that took effect in 1930. Elberfeld is now the largest municipal subdivision of Wuppertal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elberfeld” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The industrial region of <b>Elberfeld</b> and Barmen, with textile and metal trades, suffered from the Continental System, which cut it off from British markets, and conscription led to riots in 1813.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-berg">Grand Duchy of Berg</a></li></ul></section>
